## H I G H L I G H T S
G R A P H I C A L A B S T R A C T
• Effects of MOFs with organic ligands and metal nodes on PFOS adsorption were investigated.   
• The length of the organic ligand with larger 1D channel could improve PFOS adsorption performance.   
• Effect of the metal nodes was mainly relative to the unsaturated metal active sites.   
• The $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ coexistence improved PFOS removal owing to the bridging by DFT calculation.
## A R T I C L E I N F O
## A B S T R A C T
Keywords:   
Perfluorooctane sulfonate   
Metal-organic framework   
Adsorption   
DFT calculation   
Mechanism
The metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) with different metal nodes and organic ligands have been prepared and used to remove perfluorooctane sulfonate (PFOS) from water. The results show that among three metal nodes of the MIL-53(Al), MIL-53(Fe) and MIL-101(Cr), MIl-53(Al) exhibit the best PFOS adsorption performance owing to the combined action of the highest unsaturated metal active sites and the smallest pore size. MIL-53(Al)-terephthalic acid (BDC), MIL-53(Al)-dimethyl 2,6-naphthalene dicarboxylate (NDC) and MIL-53(Al)-biphenyl- $^{\cdot4,4^{\prime}}$ - dicarboxylic acid (BPDC) differs from the length of the organic ligand and MIL-53(Al)-BPDC achieves superior PFOS adsorption performance. We propose that longer organic ligand will contribute to larger 1D channel window and provide more PFOS entry. Adsorption kinetics shows that the experimental values can be well fitted to pseudo-second-order model, indicating that chemical adsorption demonstrate in the adsorption process. Besides, the existence of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ ions in MOFs/PFOS system could improve PFOS removal because of the bridging and interaction between $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ ions and the PFOS molecule, which is confirmed by the density functional theory (DFT) calculation. These optimized MOFs are hopeful adsorbents to remove PFOS from water in a sustainability perspective.
## 1. Introduction
The organic contaminants in water environment have captured lots of attentions due to the rapid development of various industries such as textile finishing, plastics, paper, cosmetics, pharmaceuticals and food processing [1–10]. Among these toxic and harmful organic pollutants, perfluorooctane sulfonate (PFOS) is a typical persistent organic pollutant. It has been found in rainwater, surface water, groundwater, and even tap water [11,12]. Owing to the high bond energy of C-F chains, PFOS has high chemical stability that makes it extreme persistent, bioaccumulative, strong toxicity and ubiquitously distributed in aquatic environments [13]. The extensive consumption of PFOS causes longterm harm to animals and human beings [14–19]. Thereby it is very important to develop effective technologies for PFOS removal [20–25], including adsorption [26,27], coagulation [28], membrane technology [29–31], thermal treatment [32], photocatalysis [33], advanced oxidation [34] and so on. Among these methods, adsorption has advantages of simple, low cost and efficient [35–39]. Therefore, it has been considered as one of the effective methods for removing PFOS [40–44]. The conventional adsorbents for the PFOS removal mainly included activated carbons [45], ion exchange resins [46], carbon nanotubes [47], clays [48], chitosan etc [49]. Actually, the choice of absorbents is the core of adsorption process.
Recently, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) have aroused significant interest in environment fields as a novel class of potential materials. Their tunable pore size, huge surface area and great porosity can be adjusted with appropriate metal ions and organic ligands [50–58]. MOFs with these excellent physical properties would have good application prospect superior adsorptive uptake in contrast to their corresponding bulk-type MOF. Heretofore, a variety of MOFs with the ability to remove pollutants from water have been reported as promising adsorbents. Xu et al. [59] prepared the 2D zinc-based MOF nanosheets to capture heavy metals in the aquatic system. Vellingiri et al. [60] using four materials to remove the formaldehyde in aqueous environments.
However, limited studies have reported on applying MOFs as adsorbent to remove PFOS from water. The very little information is available about the effect of different metal nodes and organic ligands on PFOS removal performance. Moreover, the interaction between PFOS molecules and inorganic ions need to be further investigated on the molecular level, and the molecular simulation can be carried out to gain a deeper understanding of the interaction and mechanism.
Herein, MIL-based MOFs with different metal nodes and organic ligands are used for removal of PFOS in water environment. MIL53(Al)-BPDC is well tuned and achieves superior adsorption performance. We demonstrate that both metal nodes and organic ligands can influence the size of channels and windows, which plays a key role for PFOS adsorption. Chemical adsorption is confirmed to dominate in the adsorption process and monolayer adsorption occurs on the surface of adsorbents. In addition, experimental results and density function theory (DFT) calculation indicate that the existance of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ ions in MILbaesd MOFs/PFOS system can improve the removal of PFOS. This study dedicates to optimize the structure of MOFs for a better PFOS adsorption performance.
## 2. Material and methods
## 2.1. Materials
Potassium perfluorooctane sulphonate $(\mathrm{PFOS},>99\%)$ was purchased from AccuStandard (New Haven, USA). Optima LC/MS-grade ammonium acetate, methanol $(>99\%)$ and acetonitrile $(>99\%)$ were bought from J&K Scientific (Beijing, China) and Thermo Fisher Scientific (Shanghai, China). $\mathsf{A l}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}9\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ $(>99\%)$ ), $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ $(>99\%)$ , $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}9\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ $(>99\%)$ ), hydrofluoric acid $(>99\%)$ , terephthalic acid (BDC) $(>99\%)$ ), dimethyl 2,6-naphthalene dicarboxylate (NDC) $(>99\%)$ , biphenyl $^{-4,4^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylic acid (BPDC) $(>99\%)$ and N, N-dimethylformamide (DMF) $(>99\%)$ were supplied by Sinopharm Chemical Reagent (Beijing, China) with analytical grade. Ultrapure water was provided by the MilliQ water system (Milli-Q Advantage A10, Millipore, USA) with a resistivity of $18.2~\mathrm{M}\Omega^{.}\mathrm{cm}$ . The standard PFOS was dissolved in LC/MS-grade methanol for further usage.
## 2.2. Synthesis of MOFs
MIL-53(Al), MIL-53(Fe) and MIL-101(Cr) were synthesized as follows: 17 mmol of $\mathsf{A l}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}9\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ and $9\mathrm{mmol}$ of BDC were well-mixed with $25\mathrm{mL}$ deionized water, 1 mmol $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ and 1 mmol BDC were added into ${\textsf{5}}\mathrm{mL}$ DMF mixed well, $1\ \mathrm{mmol}$ BDC and 1 mmol Cr $(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}9\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ were well-mixed with $0.2\mathrm{\mL}$ hydrofluoric acid and $4.8~\mathrm{\mL}$ deionized water in a conical flask, then the mixture was transferred into a reaction still to react at $220^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 3 days, $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 3 days and $220^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{8\mathrm{~h~}}$ , respectively. After cooling down to room temperature, the product was purified with deionized water and methanol, then all the final products were collected respectively after drying at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
MIL-53(Al)-dimethyl 2,6-naphthalene dicarboxylate (MIL-53(Al)- NDC) and MIL-53(Al)-biphenyl $^{-4,4^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylic acid (BPDC) were synthesized as follows: 1 mmol $g$ of NDC and 1 mmol of BPDC were added into $30~\mathrm{mL}$ DMF respectively. Subsequently, $1.4\mathrm{\mmol}$ of Al $(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}9\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ was added to the above solutions. The well-mixed solutions were transferred into $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ Teflon-lined stainless-steel reactors. After reacting in the oven at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ , the reactors were cooled to room temperature. The final MIL-53(Al)-MOFs were obtained after washing by DMF and methanol, and dried in the vacuum oven at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
## 2.3. Characterization
The surface area of the MOFs was conducted using an accelerated surface and porosimetry system (Micromeritics, ASAP 2020 Plus HD88, USA). A Hitachi S-3000N scanning electron microscope (SEM, Hitachi SU-8020, Japan) was used to observe the morphology of the adsorbents. The crystalline structure was determined by the X-ray diffractometer (XRD, Bruker D8 focus, USA). The X-ray photo-electron spectroscopy (XPS, Thermo ESCALAB250Xi, USA) was applied to analyze the chemical valence, and C 1s spectra was applied to analyze elemental composition.
## 2.4. Adsorption experiments
The adsorption experiments were conducted in $250~\mathrm{\mL}$ polypropylene centrifuge tubes with required concentration. After adding adsorbent, the tubes were shaken at $180\mathrm{rpm}$ and kept at $20^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ . After each adsorption experiment, the solution was filtered by a $0.22~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ membrane and kept for subsequent analysis. In another experiment, to investigate the effect of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ , $1\ \mathrm{mmol}/\mathrm{L}\ \mathrm{FeCl}_{3}$ $(99\%$ , Fisher Scientific) was added into PFOS solution at $\mathrm{\pH}=7$ , $20~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The concentration of PFOS was detected on a ultra performance liquid chromatography-tandem mass spectrometer (UPLC-MS/MS) by an UPLC system (Waters Corp., USA), which was equipped with a C18 column (BEH, particle size $1.7~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}.$ , $2.1\ \times\ 150\ \mathrm{mm}$ i.d.) and an MS system Quattro Premier XE tandem quadrupole mass spectrometer (Waters Corp., USA) with an electrospray ionization source. The mobile phase was made up of a binary mixture of (a) $10\ \mathrm{mmol/L}$ ammonium acetate in MilliQ water and (b) $0.2\mathrm{mL/min}$ acetonitrile with the ratio of 3:7. The collision energy and cone voltage were 11 ev and $30~\mathrm{V}$ , respectively. The analytes $\left(m/z\right.=\left.499.05\right.$ for PFOS) were quantified by the ion monitoring (SIM) model.
## 2.5. Density functional theory (DFT) calculation
The DFT calculation was performed using Gaussion03 (Revision C.01wis2, Gaussian, Inc., Wallingford, CT). The Becke three-parameter nonlocal exchange functional [61], the Lee–Yang–Parr correlation functional (i.e., B3LYP) [62] and $6{\cdot}311\mathsf{G}+(\mathsf{d},\mathsf{p})$ [63,64] basis set were used to optimize the gradient-corrected DFT calculation. The B3LYP functional combined with the $6{\cdot}311\mathsf{G}+(\mathsf{d},\mathsf{p})$ basis set results was the option for the ab into modeling of PFOS, and the default (75,302) p grid of Gaussian 03 gave an appropriate combination of accuracy [65]. To confirm the optimized structures to be ground states the frequencies were used without imaginary frequency.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. The effect of metal nodes of MOFs on PFOS adsorption performance
The specific surface area and average pore diameter of different MOFs are shown in Table 1.
The SEM and XRD results are shown in Fig. 1. MIL-53(Al) showed a blocky and lamellar morphology (Fig. 1a), while MIL-53(Fe) (Fig. 1b) was a rod-like crystallite. MIL-101(Cr) (Fig. 1c) had scattered octahedral shapes. The three MOF particles all displayed a regular crystal structure that was consistent with the reported data [66]. Moreover, MIL-53(Al)-NDC (Fig. 1d) and MIL-53(Al)-BPDC (Fig. 1e) showed a bulky structure with a smaller crystal size [67]. The diffraction peaks of the XRD pattern (Fig. 1 f) for all synthesized MOFs were in good agreements to the literature results [68–71]. Therefore, the five MOFs were successfully synthesized.
The change of the metal nodes (Fig. 2) would affect the coordination and spatial structure. The metal ions will coordinate with the oxygen on the organic ligands to form a complex spatial structure. We speculate that different metal ions with different spatial structure will have an impact on the adsorption efficiency.
All three types of MOFs materials have terephthalic acid as organic ligands. MIL-53(Al) and MIL-53(Fe) are very similar in their spatial structure. The central metal ions form a regular octahedron by sharing their apex, and regular octahedrons are connected through $\upmu_{2}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{OH}$ to form a train. Adjacent trains linked through terephthalic acid, which build a three-dimensional spatial structure. Meanwhile, a one-dimensional channel shaped like a rhombus could be formed to increase the access of adsorbate. The central metal ion of $\mathbf{MIL-}101(\mathrm{Cr})$ is also a regular octahedron by sharing their apexes, regular octahedrons are connected by $\upmu_{3}–0$ . Trivalence metal ion and terephthalic acid exhibit a regular tetrahedron as its apex and arris, respectively. This structure stretches in the space and form two kinds of mesoporous cages which appears alternately. The small one has pentagonal windows while the big one exhibits two kinds of windows whose shapes are pentagon and larger hexagon separately. The number of the windows directly affect the possibility of the adsorbate entering into the channel.
The adsorption performance of PFOS by MIL-53(Al), MIL-53(Fe) and MIL-101 $(\mathrm{Cr})$ with different $C_{0}$ at $\mathrm{\ttpH}=7$ is shown in Fig. 3.
It can be seen that three different central-metal-ion materials have apparently different adsorption capacity for PFOS, and the sequence is MIL-53(Al) $>$ MIL-53(Fe) $>$ MIL-101(Cr). These results are also commendably relative to the unsaturated metal sites, the specific surface area and pore size of these materials. The MIL-53(Al) with the highest unsaturated metal sites, the biggest surface area and the smallest pore diameter shows the best PFOS adsorption among them.
## 3.2. The effect of organic ligands of MOFs on PFOS adsorption performance
To further investigate the adsorption performance based on organic ligands with different length, MIL-53(Al)-BPDC, MIL-53(Al)-NDC and MIL-53(Al) with different C0were used as adsorbents to remove PFOS.
From Fig. 4A we can see that the adsorption amount of PFOS increases sequentially in the order of BDC, NDC and BPDC as organic ligands, and the adsorption amount of BDC as organic ligands is dramatically lower than other materials. The adsorption capacity of BPDC on PFOS is higher. The adsorption capacity is proportional to the length of organic ligands.
Fig. 4B shows molecular structure of three organic ligands. Because three materials use the same metal nodes as the core, pore channel is mainly dominated by organic ligands to form 1D channels of the window, the length of organic ligands directly affects the size of pore window in MOFs material. Owing to BDC as the skeleton window area is smaller than others, PFOS is relatively difficult to enter the hole with the MOF with BDC as the organic ligands. The internal pore space material has not been efficiently used, thereby greatly reducing the capability of adsorption. These observations are consistent with the result a longer organic ligand could contribute to a larger 1D channel window. The size control of channel windows eventually promotes the diffusion of PFOS into channels, which results in better adsorption ability.
In actual water, there are some cations which could influence the adsorption process. Therefore, as a common cation, $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ ion was used as a representative to explore the effect of cation on the adsorption performance. Fig. 5 reveals that the presence of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ ions with different $C_{0}$ can improve the PFOS adsorption capacity than PFOS without $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ ions. And adsorption capacity improves with the increase of the $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ concentration. It might be that the $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ ion can bridge and interact with PFOS molecular increasing the adsorption capability.
## 3.3. Adsorption kinetics
MIL-53(Al), MIL-53(Al)-NDC, MIL-53(Al)-BPDC were selected to investigate adsorption
kinetics.
Fig. 6 shows the adsorption kinetics of PFOS on MIL-53(Al)-BPDC, MIL-53(Al)-NDC and MIL-53(Al). The adsorption process obviously contains two processes. In the primary section, the adsorption rate is very high comparing to conventional adsorbents such as resins and mineral materials. The amount of PFOS adsorption increases quickly, in the initial $80~\mathrm{min}$ , and the adsorption amount could achieve approximately $80\%$ of the total amount. Then, the adsorption rate gradually decreases over time, adsorption goes into slow step and arrives to the equilibrium near $200\ \mathrm{min}$ . To further understand the adsorption kinetics, the pseudo-first-order kinetic model and the pseudo-secondorder kinetic model are selected to fit the data of the kinetic experiments.
The pseudo-first-order model presumes adsorption is controlled by diffusion, the adsorption rate is proportional to the difference between the equilibrium amount and the adsorption amount at time $t,$ which is expressed as following:
$$
\frac{d\boldsymbol{q}_{t}}{d t}=k_{1}(\boldsymbol{q}_{e}-\boldsymbol{q}_{t})
$$
$$
\ln(q_{e}-q_{t})=\ln q_{e}-k_{1}t
$$
Therefore, $k_{1}$ can be obtained as the negative number of the slope of a plot of $\ln(q_{e}-q_{t})$ versus $t$ while $q_{e}$ can be obtained since $\ln q_{e}$ is the intercept of the same plot.
The pseudo-second-order kinetic model assumes that the adsorption rate is controlled by chemical adsorption and adsorption capacity is proportional to the number of active sites on the adsorbent, which is expressed as following:
$$
\frac{d q_{t}}{d t}=k_{2}(q_{e}-q_{t})^{2}
$$
or
$$
\frac{t}{q_{t}}=\frac{1}{k_{2}{q_{e}}^{2}}+\frac{t}{q_{e}}
$$
where $q_{e}$ denotes the amount adsorbed at equilibrium $\mathbf{(mg/g)}$ , $q_{t}(\mathbf{mg}/\mathbf{g})$ is the amount adsorbed at time t (min); $k_{2}$ represents the adsorption rate constant of pseudo-second-order kinetic model $({\mathrm{g/mg}}{\mathrm{min}})$ . $q_{e}$ and $k_{2}$ can be obtained from the intercept and slope.
As shown in Fig. S1 and Table S1, the pseudo-second-order model is fitting the data much better than the pseudo-first-order kinetic model with those three adsorbents. The parameters of the pseudo-first-order kinetic model in Table S1 describes a good agreement with the adsorption conditions of all three adsorbents, such as a high relative coefficient $(\mathbb{R}^{2})>0.999$ , which indicates that chemical adsorption controls the adsorption rate in adsorption processes [72–74]. Besides, each equilibrium amount is also fitting well with the experiment data.
## 3.4. Adsorption isotherm
The adsorption isotherm experiments were performed in $50~\mathrm{mL}$ PP tubes containing $10~\mathrm{mg}$ of adsorbents and $40~\mathrm{mL}$ of solution with different PFOS concentrations $\left(5{-}100\ \mathrm{mg/L}\right)$ . Then, PP tubes were shaken at $180~\mathrm{rpm}$ and kept at $20~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 24 in a shaker. The solution $\mathsf{p H}$ was adjusted to $7.0~\pm~0.2$ . After the adsorption experiments, the mixture was filtrated with the $0.22\upmu\mathrm{m}$ PP membrane, and was stored at $15~\mathrm{mL}$
C. Zhao, et al.
PP tube for further analysis.
Fig. S2 shows the adsorption isotherms of PFOS on MIL-53(Al)- BPDC, MIL-53(Al)-NDC and MIL-53(Al) under different initial concentrations. The adsorption capacity of PFOS on three adsorbents increased with the equilibrium concentration. The adsorption saturation approached to saturate when the equilibrium concentration increased to a certain value, and then the adsorption capacity increased slowly until no increased adsorption was observed. The adsorption amounts of PFOS on three adsorbents were $\mathrm{\backslash{ML}\mathrm{{-}}53(A l)}\ <\ \mathrm{{MIL}}\mathrm{{-}}53(A l){\mathrm{-NDC}}$ $\angle\cdot\angle$ MIL-53(Al)-BPDC. The equilibrium concentration of MIL-53 (Al) and MIL-53 (Al)-NDC was about $15\mathrm{mg/L}$ , while this value was much lower than that required by MIL-53(Al)-BPDC.
To compare the adsorption capacity and adsorption characteristics of the three adsorbents, three commonly used equations, the Langmuir, Freundlich and Temkin models were applied to describe the adsorption isotherms. The three equations can be expressed as follows, respectively [41].
Langmuir model:
$$
q_{e}=q_{\mathrm{max}}{\frac{k L C e}{1+k L C e}}
$$
Freundlich mod6el:
$$
q_{e}=k_{F}C e^{\frac{1}{n}}
$$
Temkin model:
$$
q_{e}=A+B\ln C_{e}
$$
where $q_{e}$ is the adsorption amount at equilibrium $\mathbf{(mg/g)}$ , $c_{e}$ is the equilibrium concentration of PFOS in solution (mg/L), $q_{\mathrm{max}}$ is the maximum adsorption capacity $\mathbf{(mg/g)}$ , $k_{L}$ is the Langmuir adsorption constant $(\mathrm{L}/\mathrm{mg}),k_{F}$ is the Freundlich adsorption constant $(\mathrm{mg}^{1-1/\mathrm{n}}\mathrm{g}^{-1}L^{1/}$ n), $n$ is the constant depicting the adsorption intensity, $A$ and $B$ are the constants of Temkin.
The adsorption isotherms of PFOS on the three adsorbents are shown in Fig. S3. It is revealed that Langmuir model can be efficiently used to fit the adsorption of PFOS on MIL-53(Al) and MIL-53(Al)-NDC under $\mathrm{pH}=7.0$ , followed by Temkin and Freundlich models according to the correlation coefficients of $R^{2}$ (Table S2). Freundlich equation has the highest value of $R^{2}$ in fitting the adsorption of PFOS on MIL-53(Al)- BPDC (Table S2), followed by Langmuir and Temkin equations. It is noteworthy that the value of $k_{F}$ representing adsorption capacity in Freundlich model for MIL-53(Al)-BPDC is lower than that for MIL53(Al)-NDC, which is inconsistent with the experimental data.
Therefore, Langmuir and Temkin equations are more suitable to fit the adsorption isotherms of PFOS on the three adsorbents, under the condition of $\mathrm{pH}=7.0\$ , rather than Freundlich model. Previous studies demonstrated that the Langmuir equation can well describe the adsorption behavior of PFOS [38], while the Freundlich equation provides better fit to the experimental data [39,40]. Meanwhile, the Langmuir and Freundlich equations were also reported that they could provide good fitness in the adsorption of PFOS on different adsorbents [41,42]. Accordingly, it can be indicated that the adsorption behavior might be the monolayer adsorption occurring on the surfaces of monolayer with identical sites [43].
In Table S2, the maximum adsorption capacity of PFOS on the three adsorbents followed the order of $\mathrm{\Phi_{qmax}(M I L-53(A l){\cdot}B P D C)\ >\ \mathrm{\Phi_{qmax}(M I L-\Omega}}$ 53(Al)-NDC) $>$ qmax(MIL-53(Al). Compared to these adsorbents, the maximum adsorption capacities and equilibrium time of MIL-53(Al)- BPDC and MIL-53(Al)-NDC were superior than some activated carbon and minerals (Table S3). The resulting MOFs exhibit better performance, which have huge potential to be applied in the remediation of PFOS contaminations.
## 3.5. Mechanism analysis
Furthermore, to fully study the mechanism and interaction, XPS was employed to investigate the vibrational properties of the two states before and after PFOS adsorption with MIL-53(Al)-BPDC.
Fig. 7 A and B illustrate the C 1s XPS spectra of pristine MIL-53(Al)-
BPDC and used adsorption PFOS, corresponding binding energy are shown in Table S4. Comparing to the pristine MIL-53(Al)-BPDC, there appears $\mathrm{CF}_{3}$ peak at $292.9\ \mathrm{eV}$ after adsorption (Fig. 7 B), which confirms that PFOS molecules are chemically bond to the adsorbent of MOFs.
The adsorption capacity to PFOS of the three metal nodes decreases in an order of $\mathrm{MIL^{-}}53(\mathrm{Al})>\mathrm{MIL^{-}}53(\mathrm{Fe})>\mathrm{MIL^{-}}101(\mathrm{Cr}).$ As shown in Fig. 8, the unsaturated metal sites of the MOFs materials determine the adsorption polarity in the adsorption processes [75]. The aluminum in MIL-53(Al) has the highest unsaturated metal sites so that it exhibits the high adsorption affinity towards polarity molecules [71,76]. Meanwhile, MIL-53(Al) possesses more suitable pore sizes and also promotes the adsorption affinity toward PFOS [77]. The pore size of PFOS $(1.1~\mathrm{nm})$ is smaller than that of the above MOF adsorbents (Table 1). Thereupon, the adsorbent with a smaller pore size shows a higher interaction to PFOS. Overall, because of the proper pore size and coordinatively unsaturated metal active sites, the MIL-53(Al) adsorbent shows the best adsorption performance to PFOS among the three adsorbents.
In addition, DFT theory was applied to simulate the interaction and bridge of the $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ ion with the PFOS molecules, and the related geometries are shown in Fig. 9.
The structure of $\mathrm{[Fe(H_{2}O)_{6}]^{3+}}$ as the stable species in the solution is shown in Fig. 9a. A frontier orbital analysis indicated that the HOMO of the anionic PFOS surfactant is primarily localized on the sulfonate head group and as the active site binds to the positively charged $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ ion. The $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ ion can interact with one or two PFOS anions to form two following bridging structures:
$$
\mathrm{CF_{3}(C F_{2})_{7}S O_{3}\mathrm{^{-}\ +\ [F e(H_{2}O)_{6}]^{3+}\rightarrow(H_{2}O)_{4}F e-C F_{3}(C F_{2})_{7}S O_{3}\ +\ 2H_{2}O\Omega}}
$$
$$
\begin{array}{r l}&{2\mathrm{CF}_{3}(\mathrm{CF}_{2})_{7}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{\Lambda}^{-}\ +\ [\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}]^{3+}\ \rightarrow\ \mathrm{CF}_{3}(\mathrm{CF}_{2})_{7}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}\ \mathrm{-}\ \mathrm{O}_{3}\mathrm{S}}\\ &{(\mathrm{CF}_{2})_{7}\mathrm{CF}_{3}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}\ +\ 4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}(9)}\end{array}
$$
By thermodynamic analysis and DFT calculation, the configuration of $(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{4}\mathrm{FeCF}_{3}(\mathrm{CF}_{2})_{7}\mathrm{SO}_{3}$ and $\mathrm{CF}_{3}(\mathrm{CF}_{2})_{7}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\mathrm{S}(\mathrm{CF}_{2})_{7}\mathrm{CF}_{3}$ can be formed and shown in Fig. 9 c and $^{9\mathrm{~d~}}$ , respectively. The adsorption capacity of PFOS can be enhanced by adding $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ that can form complexes with PFOS. As the concentration of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ increases, the degrees of complexation and the sizes of the $\mathrm{PFOS}/\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ complexes increase [78,79]. Consequently, the adsorption affinity of PFOS improves. Moreover, $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ may neutralize the negative charge on the adsorbent surface and promotes the formation of a salt-bridge between adsorbent and PFOS [80].
Furthermore, Fig. 10 shows the pore structures and the entry of PFOS molecule into MOFs with three different organic ligands. It can be seen more clearly that the skeleton window decreases sequentially in the order of BPDC, NDC and BDC, which responds to the decrease of adsorption amount. It is consistent with the larger skeleton window of the organic ligand, the easier for the PFOS to enter into the channels. The excellent adsorption performance of MIL-53(Al)-BPDC should be attributed to the largest skeleton window, which is beneficial to PFOS entry into the channel.
## 4. Conclusions
In this work, the MOFs with different metal nodes and organic ligands as novel adsorbents have been prepared and used to remove typical PFOS from water. Among three metal nodes, MIl-53(Al) presents better PFOS adsorption performance owing to the combined action of the higher unsaturated metal active sites and the smaller pore size than MIL-53(Fe) and MIL-101 $(\mathrm{Cr})$ . MIL-based MOFs equipped with longer and larger skeleton window show a better adsorption ability which elevates the diffusion of PFOS. The PFOS adsorption capacity could be improved in the coexistence of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ ions which is arisen from the bridging and interaction between $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ ion and the PFOS molecule. This result is further proved by the DFT calculation. The optimization of MIL-based MOFs and the investigation of adsorption mechanism pave the way to fabricate better absorbents to remove persistent organic
pollutants in water treatment.
